      title: 'The Question at the Altar',
      body: 'Marcus was nineteen and had walked forward at the youth revival with his hands shaking. Afterward a deacon named Brother Odom sat with him in the fellowship hall, a paper plate of pound cake between them, and opened a Bible with a cracked spine. Brother Odom asked if he had any questions, and Marcus surprised himself. He said he had grown up two blocks from a church that his grandmother said was not for them, and he wanted to know why Jesus would be the answer for everybody if His people still sat apart on Sunday. Brother Odom laughed, not at him but with relief. He said a famous preacher once asked almost the same question on the night he was saved, and a counselor gave him a bad answer that took years to undo. So I am going to give you a better one, he said, and turned to Acts. He read "Then Peter opened his mouth, and said, Of a truth I perceive that God is no respecter of persons:" (Acts 10:34) and then the next line, "But in every nation he that feareth him, and worketh righteousness, is accepted with him." (Acts 10:35) He told Marcus that the sitting apart was real and that some of it was sin, and that the Bible would not let anyone call it holy. Then he said the hard part: you will meet Christians who get this wrong, maybe some who look like you. Do not throw away the Book because people misread it. Read it right. Marcus took the Bible home. The next Sunday he did something small. He invited the boy from the other church, the one whose father ran the hardware store, to the Wednesday study. The boy came. They argued about basketball the whole way there and did not argue at all about the verse. Brother Odom watched them come through the door and said, to nobody in particular, that this was how it always started: one question answered honestly, and one invitation sent across a street.',
