      title: 'The Assignment in the Drawer',
      body: 'Pastor Lorraine Mitchell kept a folded sheet of notebook paper in her desk drawer for eleven years. On it she had written, after a night of prayer, four words: teach them about this. This was the thing her congregation never talked about in the pulpit: how the old church on the hill had once kept a balcony for people who looked like her grandmother, and how the town still divided along the creek. She told herself the time was not right. The building fund. The new members. Who wants to bring that into a Sunday service? Then a member of the youth choir came home from a college visit crying because a campus minister had told her, kindly, that Scripture wanted the races to keep to themselves. Pastor Mitchell sat in the dark sanctuary that night and took out the paper. She thought of a preacher who had once admitted in print that he dragged his feet for years on the same kind of assignment, until something painful made him move. She did not want another child to carry a lie home because the pastor had waited for comfortable timing. The next month she began a series. She opened with "There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither bond nor free, there is neither male nor female: for ye are all one in Christ Jesus." (Galatians 3:28) and said plainly what had happened on the hill, without hatred for anyone who sat in that church now. Some families left. Two families from the church on the hill visited, and one of them stayed after service to say they were sorry for what their grandparents had allowed. She told them she forgave them before they finished the sentence, and then asked them to help her teach the next lesson. The paper is framed now, in the hallway, beside the youth choir photo.',
