      title: 'The Clip That Went Around',
      body: 'Deacon Harold Reese did not mean anything by it, he said. At the men’s breakfast someone had filmed him telling a joke about which side of town a young man should not bring a girlfriend home from, and the clip had gone around every phone in two congregations before lunch. By Sunday evening the youth pastor at Greater Mount Olive, the church across the river that shared a food pantry with his, had seen it. So had her teenagers. Pastor Denise Carter called him on Monday. She did not shout. She said, Harold, I forgive you. You have never done anything to me personally, and I would sit at your table tomorrow. But my kids heard you, and so did yours, and they heard it as the church’s word. Deacon Reese apologized to her on the phone. He meant it. Then he asked if that could be the end of it. Pastor Carter said she wished it could. She said the clip did not go to her ear alone; it went to two hundred phones. If you tell me you are sorry, I am satisfied. If you want the young people to be healed, they need to hear you say it was wrong, as loud as they heard you say it. He was angry for a week. Then he read "He that covereth his sins shall not prosper: but whoso confesseth and forsaketh them shall have mercy." (Proverbs 28:13) He stood up the next Sunday at the joint pantry service, in front of both churches, and said: I told a joke that was a lie about people Yahweh made, and I repent of it. Nobody clapped. A girl from Greater Mount Olive came over afterward and handed him a box of cans to carry. He carried it, and he came back the next Saturday to carry more.',
