      title: 'Two Different Things',
      body: 'Eight-year-old Jada knocked her cousin’s tower of blocks across the living room floor and then, under her grandmother’s eye, said sorry. Her cousin Malik, five, sniffed and said okay. Grandma Ruth said, Good. Now pick them up. Jada was outraged. I said sorry! Grandma laughed and pulled her onto the couch. Baby, she said, sorry is for his heart. Picking them up is for his tower. Malik forgave you already, look at him. But the blocks are still all over my floor. Jada looked. Malik was already building again, using only the four blocks he could reach. Grandma told her a story about a famous preacher on television who had been hurt by a friend’s words, and how he forgave the friend right away but still asked him to fix what he said, because lots of people had heard it. She said the Bible keeps both together: "Take heed to yourselves: If thy brother trespass against thee, rebuke him; and if he repent, forgive him." (Luke 17:3) Jada thought about it. Then she got down on the carpet and started handing Malik blocks, one at a time, the red ones first, because those were his favorite. Grandma Ruth watched and said, half to herself, that this was the part grown folks forget: the handing back, one block at a time, until the thing you knocked down is standing again. They built the tower taller than before. When their mothers came in from the kitchen, Malik announced that Jada had fixed it. Jada said, I said sorry AND I fixed it. Grandma Ruth said that was the whole lesson, and nobody needed a sermon after that, which was a relief to everyone.',
