      body: 'Sister Gloria had run the usher board at New Hope for twenty-two years, and she was proud of her seating chart. Front left for the mothers of the church. Front right for the deacons’ wives. The middle for families. And, without anyone ever writing it down, the back right for the new folks from the apartments on Fifth Street, most of them Haitian and Dominican, who had started coming after the food drive. One Sunday her granddaughter Kiara, visiting from college, sat in the back right on purpose and asked her afterward why that section was always the new people’s section. Gloria said that was just how it worked out. Kiara said, Grandma, that is exactly what you used to tell me about the lunch tables at my old school. Gloria was offended for a whole day. Then on Tuesday at Bible study the teacher read "My brethren, have not the faith of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Lord of glory, with respect of persons." (James 2:1) and went on to the verse about the man with the gold ring being shown a good seat and the poor man told to stand over there. Nobody looked at Gloria. Gloria looked at herself. She did not call it racism, at first. She called it habit. But the next Sunday she met the Pierre family at the door, walked them all the way down the center aisle, and sat them front left, beside Mother Holloway, who scooted over and patted the pew. Mother Holloway told Mrs. Pierre she had a beautiful hat. Mrs. Pierre told Mother Holloway that hers was more beautiful. That argument went on for three Sundays and has not been settled yet. Gloria threw out the chart and told the ushers there was one rule now: the next person in sits in the next good seat.',
