      title: 'What We Said at the Table',
      body: 'Andre did not think of himself as prejudiced. He coached youth basketball, mentored two boys from his block, and gave to the scholarship fund. But when his sister brought her new husband to Thanksgiving, a soft-spoken man from Seoul, Andre made a joke about the food, then another about driving, and the table laughed. His nine-year-old son laughed loudest. That night, putting the boy to bed, Andre heard him repeat the joke to himself and giggle. Something cold went through him. He remembered his grandfather telling him, years ago, that he had learned to fear certain neighborhoods not from anything that happened there but from things grown-ups said at the table, and that it took him half his life to unlearn it. He sat on the edge of the bed. He told his son he had said something wrong at dinner, something that made fun of a man Yahweh made and that Uncle Min did not deserve. His son asked if it was a sin. Andre opened the boy’s Bible to "If a man say, I love God, and hateth his brother, he is a liar: for he that loveth not his brother whom he hath seen, how can he love God whom he hath not seen?" (1 John 4:20) and they read it together. He said, I don’t hate Uncle Min. But I treated him like he was less than us so we could laugh, and that is the same root. The next morning Andre called his brother-in-law and apologized. Min was gracious. He said he had heard worse, and that it meant something that Andre called. At Christmas Andre asked Min to teach the boys to make dumplings, and the kitchen was loud and floury and full. His son told the whole family, unprompted, that jokes about people are not funny if they make somebody smaller.',
