      title: 'The Note in the Margin',
      body: 'When Reverend Clarence Odom died, his son Michael inherited his library, including a study Bible so worn that the spine was taped. Michael was a pastor too, at a church that had been white when his father was a boy and was now mostly Black and Latino. Turning the pages one night he found a note printed in the margin of Acts 17, under the verse about one blood, that argued for keeping the races apart. Beside it, in his father’s small handwriting, were three words: Not so. Read. And a list of references in pencil: Acts 17:26. Genesis 1:24. Deuteronomy 7:4. Ruth 1:16. Michael sat at his father’s desk and read them all. The verse the note cited said that Yahweh "hath made of one blood all nations of men" (Acts 17:26). The word kind in Genesis was about cattle and creeping things. The marriage warning in Deuteronomy gave its own reason, that Israel would be turned to other gods. And Ruth, a Moabite, came into Israel saying, thy God my God. Michael understood that his father had done quietly, for himself, what he had never said from the pulpit. He felt both proud and sad. The next Sunday he preached on testing every word by the Word. He held up the old Bible, told the congregation about the note and the three words, and said that his father had found the truth and kept it to himself, and that he would not. After service an older white man who had grown up in that church came forward and said his own father had owned the same Bible and believed that note. He asked Michael to pray with him. They prayed at the altar with the old Bible open between them.',
